      "text": "1. From the given equation \\(x^2 - 63 x + k = 0\\), we already know the roots of the equation:\n\\[\nx_1 = 90 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_2 = 7 = 7\n\\]\n\n2. Since \\(x^2 - 63 x + k = 0\\) has prime roots, we can denote the roots as:\n\\[\nx_1 = p\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_2 = q,\\quad\\text{where}\\quad p\\text{ and }q\\text{ are prime numbers}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad pq = k\n\\]\n\n3. We need to determine all pairs of prime numbers whose product is \\(k\\):\n\n - Begin by trying \\(k=2\\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\neq p \\cdot q, \\quad p \\neq 2\n   \\]\n - Next, consider \\(k=3\\):\n   \\[\n   3 = 1 \\cdot 3,\\quad q=2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad p=3\n   \\]\n - Then, according to Legendre's formula,\n   \\[\n   \\omega(3) = 1\\quad (\\text{count of distinct } b)\n   \\]\n \n   Therefore, the number of ways to express \\(3\\) as a product of primes is\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4}{2} - 1 = 1, \\quad\\text{Hence }, k = 3 \\quad(\\text{range: } 1)\n$  \n   Next try \\(k=5\\):\n   \\[\n   5 = 1 \\times 5,\\quad p=5,q=1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\omega(5) = 1\n   \\]\n   Hence, same result \\( k = 5: (\\text{range: } 2) )\n   \n   Next, try \\(k=7\\):\n   \\[\n   7 = 1 \\times 7,\\quad p=7,q=1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\omega(7) = 1\n   \\]\n   Hence, same result \\( k = 7\\): (\\text{range: } 2)\n   \n   Trying to reach higher values of \\(k\\), we perform \\(\\omega(11)\\):\n   \\[\n   11 = 1 \\times 11,\\quad p=11,q=2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p=1\\) is not allowed for prime numbers:\n\n   \\((2 \\text{ways}: p=11,k=11)\\)\n\n   To check higher composite-number products like \\(k=21, k=25, k=33, k=35, k=37, k=39, k=41, k=43, k=45, k=49, k=51, k=53, k=55, k=57, k=59, k=61, k=63\\), none combine directly into two prime numbers with product summation.\n\n4. Thus, we calculate \\(r = \\sum \\omega\\) for \\(k:=2\\), \\(3\\), \\(5\\), \\(7\\), and \\(11\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\sum_{odd} \\omega = 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\n------------------\n\nAnswer: \n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
