      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The quadratic equation with constant term $k$ and roots $x_1$ and $x_2$ can be rewritten as:\n\\[ (x - x_1)(x - x_2) = x^2 - (x_1 + x_2)x + x_1 x_2 = 0 \\]\nDue to the Vieta's formulas, we have $x_1 + x_2 = 63$ and $x_1 x_2 = k$. To satisfy both the above rules, the prime factors of 63 have to be the roots given by. The prime factors of 63 are 3 and 7. Trying all possible combinations of these roots gives us:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Prime roots} & [3,7], \\text{root product} = k = 21 \\\\\n\\text{Prime roots} & [7,3], \\text{root product} = k = 21 \\\\\n\\text{Prime roots} & [3, 63], \\text{root product} = k = 189 \\\\\n\\text{Prime roots} & [63,3], \\text{root product} = k = 189 \\\\\n\\text{Prime roots} & [7, 9], \\text{root product} = k = 63 \\\\\n\\text{Prime roots} & [9,7], \\text{root product} = k = 63 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince $k = 21 \\text{ and } k = 189$ both lead to the same result, and $k = 63$ and $k = 63$ lead to the same result, there are actually just 2 possible values of $k$ that satisfy the initial conditions.\n\nTherefore, the number of possible values for $k$ is $\\boxed{2}$.",
